## 2026-08-07 chore/add-python-linter — a linter, so reviewers stop finding what a machine finds free

  PROTECTED-PATH AUTHORITY. Two protected paths are edited: `.gitlab-ci.yml` (a PROTECTED_FILE and
  one of the three guard paths, `task_contract_gate.py:77`) and `.claude/hooks/git_discipline.py`
  (PROTECTED prefix). Both carry the opus floor.

  CPO INSTRUCTION (conversation, 2026-08-07): **"ok, do it"**, approving a plan that named the
  dependency, the `F`/`E9` ruleset, the `.gitlab-ci.yml` step and the opus floor explicitly. It
  followed his question *"What would an experienced software developer who has defined 100 AI
  guardrails do in this situation?"* and a stated recommendation to push checks down the cost curve
  rather than add more review. This entry and the contract's `protected_override` were written in
  the SAME action (#28's class fix).

  ⭐ THE ARGUMENT, WITH THE MEASUREMENT THAT MAKES IT. The CPO's constraint was explicit: mechanisms
  that build trust, WITHOUT red tape that slows work and burns tokens. The answer offered was that
  the guards are not the red tape — spending the most expensive check on facts a cheap one settles
  is. Measured on #25 the same day: `platform-reviewer` at opus spent ~125k tokens finding a
  duplicate module-level function name. That is pyflakes **F811**, reported in milliseconds. Three
  of the four defects found across #25's three rounds were mechanically decidable.

  MEASURED BEFORE THE RULESET WAS LOCKED, which the plan required so the rules could not be chosen
  to flatter the number: `ruff check . --select F,E9` reports **12** violations — 10 × F401 unused
  import, 1 × F811, 1 × F841. Small enough to fix here rather than bring back as a count.

  ⭐ THE FINDING THAT SETTLES IT. One of the 12 is `.claude/hooks/git_discipline.py:197`, an unused
  `import subprocess` **introduced by !19 earlier the same day**, when the git call moved into
  `_staged_stat`. It passed THREE review rounds and TWO opus reviewers and reached `main`. The
  linter finds it instantly. That is the case for this change stated as evidence rather than as
  argument.

  ⚠ AND THE PREMISE FAILED ITS OWN VERIFICATION, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. The plan's first
  acceptance criterion was "show ruff reporting F811 for the exact duplicate `_review_patch` that
  cost #25 a round; if it does not catch that, the linter is not worth adding." Run rather than
  assumed, DEFAULT ruff does NOT catch it: `dummy-variable-rgx` exempts underscore-prefixed names,
  so `f` and `review_patch` are flagged while `_f`, `_review_patch` and `__x` pass silently. Every
  test helper here is underscore-prefixed by convention, so the linter would have shipped INERT
  against the very class that justified it, and the MR would have read as a success.

  FIXED BY ONE SETTING, and the cost was measured rather than hoped: `dummy-variable-rgx = "^_+$"`
  restricts the exemption to bare underscores. `_review_patch` is then flagged; `for _ in range(3)`
  and `a, _ = (1, 2)` still pass; the repo-wide count stays at 12, so it adds no noise. Recorded
  because the reusable lesson is not about ruff: an acceptance criterion that is actually RUN can
  reject the premise of its own task, and this one did.

  NEW DEPENDENCY — DECLARED, and it is a CTO threshold in its own right. `ruff`, pinned in a NEW
  `requirements-dev.txt` so it cannot enter the runtime image.

  RECURRING COST — YES, DECLARED: one extra CI step per MR, seconds, no new service and no
  schedule. Set against three review rounds on #25 alone.

  BOUNDED: correctness rules only (`F` + `E9`). NO style, import-sorting or formatting rules — on
  an existing codebase those produce a large mechanical diff nobody can review, and they would turn
  a cheap win into churn. Widening the ruleset later is a fresh decision.

  ⚠ ROUND 1: THE PREMISE WAS FALSE, AND THE CHANGE WOULD HAVE LOOSENED A GUARD. `cto-reviewer` and
  `platform-reviewer`, independently at opus, both FAILed on the same thing: this repo ALREADY has
  ruff. `.pre-commit-config.yaml` configures `astral-sh/ruff-pre-commit` `rev: v0.7.4` over
  `ingestion|scripts|tests|dbt_project/macros|dbt_project/seeds`, `.git/hooks/pre-commit` is
  installed, and `README.md` documents it. The contract's "This repo has NO linter and none pinned"
  came from grepping `requirements*.txt` alone and reporting that scoped result as general — the
  #904 class, third instance from this builder in two days.

  THE CONSEQUENCE, MEASURED rather than argued: ruff walks up for config, so the NEW root
  `ruff.toml` is read by the pre-commit hook too. Over that hook's file set, ruff's defaults
  (`E4,E7,E9,F`) report **10** violations, all E402; the approved `select = ["F","E9"]` reports
  **0**. The MR would have silently stopped E4 and E7 being enforced on every local commit — a
  never-loosen-a-guard failure inside an MR whose entire justification was strengthening guards.

  CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-08-07): **"do it"**, against a recommendation to set `select` to
  ruff's DEFAULT `["E4","E7","E9","F"]` so nothing narrows anywhere, declare the 10 E402s in
  `per-file-ignores` (all four files use the deliberate `sys.path.insert`-then-import shim), align
  the pre-commit `rev` to the pinned `0.16.2` since both now read one config, and add a test pinning
  the load-bearing settings. The recommendation stated plainly that `.pre-commit-config.yaml` was
  outside the approved scope.

  ONE ROUND-1 FINDING REJECTED, with evidence. `scope-auditor` FAILed on the "12 violations" figure,
  arguing no live F811 existed. It did: `tests/test_batch_fixtures.py:464 F811 Redefinition of
  unused 'call' from line 9`. Removing `call` from the line-9 import cleared BOTH that F811 and its
  F401 in one edit, so the branch shows 11 edits for 12 violations. It counted edits, not
  violations.

  ⭐ THE REUSABLE LESSON, which is not about ruff. Two of the three round-1 FAILs trace to one
  habit: a grep scoped to where I expected the answer, reported as though it were exhaustive. The
  fix is not "grep harder" — it is that a claim of ABSENCE ("no linter exists", "nothing imports
  this") must name where it looked, so a reviewer can see the boundary and test it.

  ⚠ ROUND 2: THE TWO FAILS PULLED IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, AND THE FIX DISSOLVED BOTH.
  `cto-reviewer` FAILed the `.pre-commit-config.yaml` `rev` bump that round 1's own fix introduced:
  that `rev` also governs `ruff-format`, which rewrites files on every local commit, and across
  v0.7.4 to v0.16.2 **75 files would be reformatted** (measured with `ruff format --diff`). That is
  precisely the unreviewable mechanical diff this change argues against. But NOT aligning the
  versions is `platform-reviewer`'s round-1 finding — two ruff versions reading one config, one of
  them running `--fix`. Both moves lose.

  RESOLVED STRUCTURALLY. Ruff auto-discovers only `ruff.toml`, `.ruff.toml` and `pyproject.toml`.
  Naming the file `.ruff-ci.toml` and passing `--config` from CI means the pre-commit hook never
  reads it: no shared config, no alignment owed, `ruff-format` never moves, and neither enforcement
  point can silently change the other. VERIFIED both ways before adopting. `.pre-commit-config.yaml`
  is now byte-identical to `main` and left `scope_paths`. **The round-3 change touches strictly LESS
  than round 2 did** — the amendment narrows the task rather than widening it.

  ⚠ AND THE CORRECTION ITSELF FAILED THE SAME WAY TWICE MORE, which is the part worth keeping.
  `cto-reviewer`: round 2 corrected the false "no linter" premise in `contract.md` but left it
  standing VERBATIM in `.gitlab-ci.yml` and the config's own header — the two PERMANENT artifacts,
  where a comment is all a future engineer reads, and one of them then argued against itself
  eighteen lines later. `feedback_corrections_replace.md` is exactly this: corrections replace, they
  do not accumulate. `platform-reviewer`: `done_when` still named `ruff check . --select F,E9` as
  the acceptance command, and a CLI `--select` OVERRIDES the config file — so the stated
  verification bypassed the thing it verified and would have passed on a tree where the config
  failed to load. `scope-auditor`: "ten F401s survived the hook" contradicted its own sentence,
  since one of the ten is in `.claude/hooks/`, which the same sentence says the hook does not cover.

  ⭐ FOUR #904-CLASS CLAIMS IN TWO DAYS, THE LAST ONE INSIDE THE FIX FOR THE THIRD. Counted so the
  pattern is visible rather than reading as four unrelated slips: the false `impact_map` import
  claim (!19), the stale `done_when` test count (!19), "this repo has NO linter" (here, round 1),
  and "ten F401s" (here, round 2, written while correcting the third). Prose has not fixed it. The
  only thing that has caught every instance is a reviewer running the command — which is the
  argument for this MR, turned on its author.

