## 2026-08-08 — STANDING RULE: a reference broken by an approved change is part of that change

  CPO RULING, given 2026-08-08 on the `ci/scope-triggers-and-restore-ingest-lock` branch. A RULE
  EXTENSION (§10), so it is the CPO's and nobody else's, and it is recorded here because a rule
  that lives only in a task contract does not survive the next task.

  THE RULE, verbatim as recommended and accepted:

    "Updating a reference that an approved change itself breaks is part of that change, not a
    scope extension — provided the update is confined to the reference and changes no behaviour."

  WHAT IT PERMITS. Adding a file to `scope_paths` mid-task, without a fresh CPO grant, WHEN the
  only edit to that file is repairing a name the same task's approved change invalidated. The
  amendment is still written on a clean tree and still recorded in `amendments:`; what it no longer
  needs is a separate authority line, and it cites THIS entry instead.

  WHAT IT DOES NOT PERMIT, and these limits are the rule:
  - Any other edit to the newly in-scope file. Fix the reference and nothing else. "While I was in
    there" is exactly the drift `scope_paths` exists to stop.
  - Anything that changes behaviour. A stale COMMENT or docstring, a doc sentence, a fixture name —
    yes. A code path, an assertion, a config value — no, even if it "only" restores intent.
  - Discovering the reference and using it as a reason to widen the task's objective.
  - A PROTECTED path. `.claude/hooks/**`, `.claude/agents/**`, `.claude/commands/**`,
    `.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/review_routing.json`, `.mcp.json`, `.cursor/mcp.json`,
    `.github/workflows/**`, `.gitlab-ci.yml` still need `protected_override` and an `impact_map`,
    rule or no rule. Nothing here touches the protected-path machinery.

  WHY IT EXISTS. `scope-auditor` FAILed round 2 of this branch, correctly: the round-2 amendment
  added `tests/test_governance_hooks.py` for a ONE-WORD docstring fix — it named the `*data_paths`
  anchor the same MR renames — and justified it with "no new authority is needed to do LESS", which
  covered the item-3 WITHDRAWAL in the same amendment but not the addition. §2 says each scope
  extension records the CPO authority, and it cited on-point precedent (2026-06-23,
  `feat/player-per90-metrics`) where an equivalent stale-comment touch-up was held to need the CPO.
  The auditor was right on the letter and the finding stands.

  The CPO's answer was to fix the CLASS rather than the instance. Every rename breaks references;
  the same question was going to arrive again in Wave 2 (raw merge-on-write renames things) and
  Wave 3, and each arrival costs a review round to ask. Deciding it once is cheaper than deciding
  it five times, and leaving it undecided has a worse failure mode than either answer: a builder
  who does not want to spend a round will simply leave the stale reference, which is how a repo
  accumulates comments naming things that no longer exist.

  THE COUNTER-ARGUMENT, recorded so the rule can be attacked later. This narrows what
  `scope_paths` catches. The mitigation is that the permitted edit is defined by the change that
  precedes it — you may repair only what you broke — so the blast radius of an abuse is one wrong
  identifier in one comment, and every reviewer still sees the file in the diff. If it is ever
  abused, the fix is to withdraw the rule, not to add a gate.

  FIRST APPLICATION: this branch. `tests/test_governance_hooks.py:2040` said "data:build:main's
  `*data_paths`"; item 2 renamed that anchor to `*data_paths_prod`; the fix is that one word.

  FOLLOW-UP NOT DONE HERE, deliberately. `docs/working_agreement.md` §2 is where a reader looks for
  the amendment rules, and this ruling is not in it — adding it would itself be a scope extension
  onto a file this task has no business editing, mid-review, at round 3 of a 3-round cap. Folding
  the rule into §2 is worth its own small change.

## 2026-08-08 — #33 item 8 SPLIT into 8a/8b: item 8 as approved would have destroyed prod data

  CPO DECISION, taken 2026-08-08, AFTER and NARROWING the blanket #33 approval recorded above.
  Recorded here because that blanket entry approved item 8 as one thing ("raw merge-on-write with
  the raw_archive backup first") and this changes what item 8 IS. `scope-auditor` FAILed round 1 of
  `fix/896-guard-partial-writes` for its absence and was right — the fourth time this repo has
  ruled that a decision living only in `contract.md` is not recorded, after 2026-07-31, 2026-08-01
  and 2026-08-08 (the standing-rule entry above).

  WHAT WAS FOUND, and it is why the split exists. Item 8 converts the append-only raw tables to
  merge-on-write keyed on `league_code`. Four of the target loaders abandon their fetch loop when
  the daily quota latches and then write the partial payload unconditionally:

      transfers.py:31-33   coaches.py:44-45   standings.py:31-32   teams.py:29-30
      for x in ...:
          if errors_quota._http_quota_exhausted:
              break                      # loop abandoned, nothing recorded
          ...
      load_json_to_bq(..., append=True)  # partial written as if complete

  Under append-only that is RECOVERABLE: the partial wins the latest-snapshot `qualify` in
  staging, but the complete prior row still exists in `raw`. Under merge-on-write the partial
  write DELETES the complete prior row. A single quota cut would permanently destroy the good
  snapshot — including RAW_APIF_TRANSFERS, measured 2026-08-08 at 6.99 GiB, 59% of all raw.

  This is #896 ("an incomplete fetch must never supersede good data"), already ruled on
  2026-08-03 and already implemented for RAW_APIF_PLAYERS only. A sweep found
  `result_is_complete()` is called in exactly ONE place in the whole codebase; nine loaders
  fetch-and-write with no completeness check at all.

  THE RULING.
  - **8a** — port the #896 guard first, to `transfers`, `coaches`, and (checked, and they had the
    same defect) `standings` and `teams`. No deletion, no merge-on-write. Independently valuable,
    can only ever write FEWER rows than today, and is the precondition that makes 8b safe.
  - **8b** — merge-on-write, only for tables provably complete-snapshot AFTER 8a.
  - The CPO's words on being shown the above: "go ahead with 8a".

  SCOPE OF 8a, decided explicitly rather than inferred: FOUR loaders, not two and not nine. Two
  (`transfers`, `coaches`) were named by the CPO; `standings` and `teams` were named as "check" and
  were found to have the identical defect, so they are in. The remaining five gaps — `catalog.py`,
  `injuries.py`, `fixtures.py`, `player_profiles.py`, `player_teams.py`, plus the body-error half
  of `player_squads.py` — are NOT in 8a. They are listed in that branch's `decisions_reserved` and
  reported on #33 so 8b's scope is decided with them in view.

  A LIMIT ON 8b THAT IS ALREADY KNOWN, recorded now so it cannot be lost between MRs:
  `PLAYER_PROFILES` and `PLAYER_TEAMS` must NEVER be converted to a `league_code`-keyed delete.
  They accumulate per player — `players_needing()` writes only newly-seen players — so deleting
  prior rows for a league would erase every player ingested on an earlier run.

  ALSO CORRECTED, from the same investigation: #33's item 8 says "eight append-only raw tables"
  and "six per-league snapshot tables". The code has TEN append-only tables, and the set safe for
  a `league_code`-keyed delete is smaller than six and is not the obvious six. Do not take either
  count from #33 when scoping 8b; re-derive it from the loaders.


2026-08-09 — #33 item 8b SCOPED TO THREE TABLES (a reduction of an approved item)

  CONTEXT. The 8b scope was re-derived from the loaders and their CONSUMERS, as the 2026-08-08
  entry above requires. #33 item 8 was approved by the CPO on 2026-08-08 ("yes" to items 7, 8, 9)
  against a text naming eight tables. The set actually safe is THREE: TRANSFERS, STANDINGS, TEAMS.
  Recorded here, not only in contract.md, because scope-auditor FAILed 8a round 1 for exactly that
  omission — the fourth time this repo has ruled that a scope living only in the contract is not
  recorded.

  THE TEST APPLIED, which #33 does not state: a `league_code`-keyed delete is safe only if BOTH
  (a) the loader writes a COMPLETE per-league row each run, and (b) NO consumer reads snapshot
  history. #33 only ever considered (a). Condition (b) is what excludes COACHES.

  EXCLUDED — DATA DESTRUCTION, not preference:
  - COACHES. `stg_apif__coaches.sql:1-7` reads ALL snapshots deliberately, by the CPO ruling of
