2026-08-17 — ⭐ RECOVERY RECIPE: "GONE AT SOURCE" IS NOT "GONE". BIGQUERY TIME TRAVEL RECOVERED 12
OF THE 29 DESTROYED EVENTS

  THE CLAIM THAT STOOD FOR A WEEK, in this log, in `!57`'s detector docstring and in the handover:
  *"the backlog is UNREPAIRABLE — verified live against the provider, which no longer returns those
  events."* The verification was real and the sentence was still wrong, because it answered
  "can the PROVIDER give it back" and was used to conclude "can WE get it back". Nobody checked our
  own copy. BigQuery keeps 7 days of time travel on every table, and the pre-delete payloads were
  sitting in it the whole time.

  ⭐ THE DISTINCTION, and it is the durable lesson: **irrecoverable AT SOURCE is not irrecoverable.**
  Before writing off destroyed data, enumerate every copy that could still hold it — time travel,
  a dated archive dataset, an accumulating downstream fact, a snapshot table. Here the fact
  (`fct_fixture_event`, incremental) had been holding the events all along, which is how the loss
  was detectable at all; time travel is what made it FIXABLE.

  WHAT WAS MEASURED, per fixture, `n_events` in raw:
      fixture   now  recoverable  lost   outcome
      1490377    17      18        1     RESTORED
      1507028    15      16        1     RESTORED
      1564795    17      27       10     RESTORED  (the penalty shootout)
      1564791     4       4        4     gone — richer payload deleted before 2026-08-10 09:46
      1564793     2       2       13     gone — same
  12 recovered, 17 permanently lost because their deletes fell outside the 7-day window. The
  recovery ran with roughly 30 hours to spare on 1564795.

  THE RECIPE, and each step has a trap in it:
  1. FIND THE LOSS from the fact, not from raw:
       select fact.fixture_sk, count(*) from core.fct_fixture_event fact
       left join dbt_analytics.base_apif__fixture_events b
         on fact.fixture_sk=b.fixture_id and fact.event_index=b.event_index
       where b.fixture_id is null group by 1
  2. PROBE the window for a richer payload. ⚠ One `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` per table per query —
     BigQuery rejects two different timestamps against the same table, so probe one point at a
     time. Bisect: the delete's own `ingested_at` tells you when the replacement landed.
  3. STAGE it. ⚠ You CANNOT read a table `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` and use it as a DML destination in
     the same statement ("referenced with multiple snapshot read timestamps"). Write the recovered
     rows to a scratch table first, then INSERT from there.
  4. APPEND, and ⭐ **PRESERVE THE ORIGINAL `ingested_at`.** This is the step that makes it correct
     rather than destructive. Base dedups newest-per-entity-key, so the restored older row supplies
     only the entities the newer payload LACKS and never overwrites a corrected value. Stamping it
     `now()` would invert that and make the stale payload win everywhere.
  5. VERIFY BY SIMULATING BASE before rebuilding anything — unnest both rows,
     `qualify row_number() over (partition by fixture_id, event_index order by raw_ingested_at
     desc)=1`, count. Confirmed 18/16/27 before a single dbt model ran.

  ⚠ THIS ONLY WORKS BECAUSE RAW IS NOW APPEND-ONLY. Under merge-on-write the restored row would
  have been deleted by the next retry, and appending a second version would have been meaningless.
  `!59` is what made the repair safe; the repair is the first production proof that the rule works.

  MEASURED OUTCOME, end to end:
      prod build      PASS=745 ERROR=1 SKIP=103   ->  PASS=849 ERROR=0 SKIP=0
      dim_player orphans            1             ->  0
      fixture 1564795 events        17            ->  27
      `!57` pipeline               red            ->  green
  The single orphan was fixture 1564795 / event_index 25 / player 544602 (G. Arditi, a shootout
  goal). Restoring that one payload cleared the orphan, which unskipped 103 nodes, which resolved
  `assert_momentum_window_matches_momentum` (2514 rows and growing) on every MR.

  ⚠ DEADLINE ARITHMETIC, for the next time: a delete at time T is recoverable until T+7d. Compute
  the expiry the moment you find the loss and say it out loud, because the window closes silently.
  ⚠ `!57`'s kickoff cutoff STAYS as shipped: 17 events on 1564791/1564793 are still gone, so
  lowering it would redden every build on damage nobody can fix.


