2026-08-16 feat/69-5-62-3-country-fk-and-mart - the competitions-page ordering rule, sort_order
obsolete (#54).

  ⚠ MISSING AGAIN, THIRD TIME ON THIS ISSUE PAIR, AND scope-auditor CAUGHT IT AGAIN. The two
  entries above record the #69 model rescope. Neither records this separate ruling, made the same
  day on #54: the competitions page's ROW ORDER. The authority existed only as a note I (a prior
  session) wrote on GitLab #54, cited from `mart_competition_index.sql`'s header comment and
  `contract.md`'s decisions_taken with no escalations.log entry to back it. scope-auditor FAILED
  the `feat/69-5-62-3-country-fk-and-mart` branch for exactly that, searching this file for
  "region_rank", "sort_order", "obsolete" and every 2026-08-16 entry and finding nothing. Same
  class as the entry above (`feedback_fix_the_class_not_the_instance`): a GitLab issue note is not
  this log, and that lesson evidently needs a THIRD instance before it generalises.

  THE ASK. Note 3 on #54 built a provenance table naming `.sort_order` as the row-order source.
  Reading the registry's actual `sort_order` values showed they were not a coherent rank: values
  restart in some competition_types, run through in others, collide across them (110/120/130
  appear twice), with unexplained gaps.

  RULING 1 - SORT_ORDER IS OBSOLETE. CPO: "sort order is obsolete. The ranking has to be flexible
  depending on kickoff times of the upcoming matches, so even matches from national teams can rank
  occasionally before club league matches... a rule that uses kick-off date and a judgement
  regarding ranking of regions (continents)." The registry's hand-typed `sort_order` is retired as
  an ordering basis; `mart_competition_index` does not carry it.

  RULING 2 - THE APPROVED SORT KEY. Proposed and approved: has-an-upcoming-fixture, then days to
  next kickoff BUCKETED BY CALENDAR DAY (not by timestamp — a raw-clock tiebreak would rank the
  Eredivisie above the Premier League for kicking off at 10:15 instead of 19:00, which is noise),
  then `region_rank`, then next kickoff time, then `league_code`. Competitions with nothing
  upcoming sort last, most-recently-played first. MEASURED before proposing (`bq query --dry_run`,
  755,625 bytes, whole population, 2026-08-16): 25 of 45 competitions have an upcoming fixture, 20
  do not — the no-fixture branch is 44% of the page, not an edge case; 11 kick off the same day, so
  region_rank is not a rare tiebreak.

  RULING 3 - REGION_RANK, THE ONE HAND-SET INPUT. Proposed and CPO-approved: UEFA=1, FIFA=2,
  CONMEBOL=3, CONCACAF=4, AFC=5, CAF=6, OFC=7. A JUDGEMENT, not geography, and his to change —
  landed on `confederations.csv`, the seed that already records `FIFA -> World` as the same class
  of judgement.

  RULING 4 - THE MART CARRIES FACTS, THE SPEC DECLARES THE ORDER BY. CPO, correcting a draft that
  wanted a precomputed `group_sort_order` column baked into the mart: "I don't agree that sorting
  has to be decided in the mart." Sorting is arrangement, not a fact a visitor reads, so
  `mart_competition_index` carries `next_kickoff_datetime`, `last_kickoff_datetime` and
  `region_rank` as facts and does not pre-bake the ORDER BY — the page spec (#62 step 5) declares
  it. `region_label` is the opposite case and DOES resolve in the mart, because it is text a
  visitor reads.

2026-08-18 feat/shared-competition-order — TWO rulings, in conversation, written the same session
they were made (the 2026-08-16 entry above was missed three times before it got recorded; this one
is not repeating that).

  CONTEXT. Walking the home page top-to-bottom against the mock, the CPO asked whether the
  "next matches" ordering rule was consistent across every page it applies to. It was not. Two
  rules were live at once: the competitions index used the 2026-08-16 key (day → region_rank →
  kickoff → league_code); the home page, built earlier under #367, used raw chronology and so still
  carried the exact 10:15-beats-19:00 noise the 08-16 ruling was written to remove.

  RULING 1 — ONE SHARED RULE, SITE-WIDE. CPO: "yes, make it one shared rule." The 2026-08-16 key is
  now the single rule wherever competitions are ranked against each other, implemented ONCE
  (`site_v2/src/lib/competitionOrder.mjs`) and called by both surfaces. Not a new key and not a
  re-decision: the 08-16 ruling's own words ("even matches from national teams can rank
  occasionally before club league matches") already reached the home page, which simply predated
  it. Because DAY precedes REGION in the key, match SELECTION stays chronological and only GROUP
  ORDER changes — the CPO's question "I don't understand your question" correctly identified that
  the key already answers whether region can outrank an earlier day. It cannot.

  RULING 2 — THE HARDCODED COUNT OF 12 IS RETIRED. CPO: "why are you using hard-coded numbers like
  12 matches or whatsoever? We will show what we have, more matches will come, because we ingest
  more competitions." `_HERO_FIXTURE_LIMIT = 12` is deleted. The block now shows THE NEXT MATCHDAY:
  every fixture on the earliest upcoming kickoff date.

  ⚠ The count was NOT unreasoned — `10_home.md` measured that twelve filled the first screenful
  across two or three competitions on every day sampled. It fails on a different axis: it does not
  SCALE. As competitions are onboarded, twelve slots hold fewer and fewer of them, so the block
  narrows exactly as the site broadens. Measured on the committed sample: 12 fixtures spanning TWO
  dates, which is not a matchday and reads as an arbitrary cut.

  ⚠ This does NOT reopen what GAP-02 settled. "Today's matches" was rejected for rendering one row
  on some days measured; the rule adopted is "the earliest date PRESENT IN THE DATA", which is
  never empty by construction. The per-day measurement table stays in `10_home.md` as the evidence
  for that distinction.

  ⚠ HONEST UPPER BOUND, stated rather than designed around: the busiest day in that measurement
  carried 57 fixtures. If a busy matchday reads too long, the answer is a CPO decision on the block
  (#908 already parks a "more matches" control) — NOT a new number quietly reintroduced. Recorded
  in the contract's decisions_reserved so the next session cannot treat re-capping as a detail fix.

  RULING 3 — THE LAYER CLASSIFICATION, escalated after TWO reviewer FAILs and ruled by the CPO.

  THE DISPUTE. analytics-engineer-reviewer FAILed the matchday selection twice. Round 1: computing
  it with a Python `min()` over fetched rows is window selection, forbidden in the consumption
  layer, and the same shape as `_featured_season_row` (#846), which was fixed by computing
  `is_featured_season` as a TESTED MART COLUMN and leaving the export only filtering on it. I moved
  the selection into the export's own SQL (`where fixture_date = (select min(fixture_date) from
  upcoming)`), the option the round-1 finding itself offered. Round 2 it FAILed again on stronger
  ground: `layering.md` binds the FILE, not the language, so SQL authored in `export_site_data.py`
  is still the export deciding; only the syntax moved, and no served fact exists.

  THE BUILDER'S COUNTER, recorded because it is not obviously wrong: the same `WHERE` clause has
  carried `status_short in ('NS','TBD') and fixture_date >= current_date()` since #367 — also a
  window, never flagged — and the contract explicitly ALLOWS "select, filter". Whether
  `fixture_date = (select min(...))` is an allowed filter or a forbidden window selection is
  genuinely ambiguous, and `working_agreement.md` §10 makes an unclear layer classification the
  CPO's call rather than the builder's or a reviewer's.

  CPO ANSWER (2026-08-18): **"ship as-is, register the gap."** Shipped with the SQL selection;
  registered as **GAP-32**. ⚠ This is a decision to PROCEED, not a finding that the reviewer was
  wrong — the entry and the gap both record the dispute so the next session inherits it instead of
  rediscovering it. The proper fix needs a warehouse home for upcoming fixtures that does not exist
  (the hero reads `core.fct_fixture` directly, itself a flagged defect); the two should be fixed
  together, as their own task.

  ⚠ ONE PART OF THE FINDING WAS UNCONTESTED AND IS FIXED, not shipped under the ruling: the test I
  wrote to pin the placement asserted `"min(fixture_date)" in inspect.getsource(...)` — a grep
  dressed as a test, which would have pinned the disputed placement in place. DELETED rather than
  relaxed, with the reason written where it stood.

2026-08-18 chore/record-top-players-ruling — the Top players block: ONE PER LEAGUE, not a pooled
ranking. Recorded BEFORE any build, which is the point.

  HOW IT SURFACED. Walking the home page block by block, the CPO looked at the Top players mock and
  asked: "what is happening here? We show the no1 from each league and then we put all these no1s
  into one view -> do we really need a separate mart for it, we can build it but it's repetitive
  work, not sure about it".

  ⚠ THE MOCK AND THE SPEC DISAGREED, AND NOBODY HAD NOTICED. Measured: all four boards render 7
  rows with 7 DISTINCT leagues, so the mock reads as one-per-league. But `10_home.md` says "pooled
  across the leagues of one pool", and `gen_top_players.py`'s own note says "a pooled board ranks
  across seven leagues, so the club alone no longer implies the competition" — a sentence that only
  makes sense if one league can appear twice. The mock's INVENTED numbers happened to produce a
  balanced one-per-league board, which read as a rule it was never stating. A placeholder dataset
  can assert a design nobody chose; that is the durable lesson here.

  RULING 1 — ONE PER LEAGUE. CPO: "One per league -> yes, it's not a leaderboard in the defined
  pool." Each pool league's rank-1 player on the metric, collected, then ordered by value.

  WHAT THIS COSTS AND SAVES. It answers the CPO's own mart question: NO new mart and NO new
  ranking. `mart_leaderboards` already ranks within (league_code, season_api_year), so each
  league's number one already exists — the block filters to rank 1 across the pool's leagues.
  **GAP-31 (a pooled rank across the pool) is WITHDRAWN by this ruling**, not deferred. Still live
  and untouched by it: GAP-30 (`assists` is not a ranked board at all, so it has no rank-1 to
  take), GAP-27 (no club on the row), GAP-28 (pool membership).

  RULING 2 — THE INTRO COPY MUST BE REPHRASED, and the words are the CPO's. "Season totals to date.
  Ranked across pooled leagues: Premier League, La Liga, …" describes the pooled ranking just ruled
  out. CPO: "we just need to rephrase". ⚠ NOT WRITTEN HERE — copy is §10 and always his. What the
  sentence must carry factually: each league's leader, season-to-date, and which leagues.

  ⚠ NOT DECIDED, do not assume either way: whether ordering the seven winners by value lives in the
  mart or the page. The 2026-08-16 Ruling 4 ("the mart carries facts, the spec declares the ORDER
  BY") points at the page, but it was ruled about competitions, not players.

2026-08-18 chore/record-top-teams-ruling — the Top teams block: ONE PER LEAGUE, same as players.
Recorded BEFORE any build, which is the point.

  HOW IT SURFACED. Continuing the same block-by-block home page audit, the same "one per league?"
  question that was asked and ruled for Top players (above) was still unasked for Top teams. Before
  asking it, `mart_team_competition_benchmarks` was checked (already ranks per (league_code,
  season_api_year, metric_key), same shape `mart_leaderboards` has for players) and
  `top_teams_mock.html` was opened per THE METHOD (check the mock's numbers, don't trust them).

  ⚠ THE MIRROR IMAGE OF THE PLAYERS FINDING. For players, the mock's invented numbers happened to
  look one-per-league by accident while the spec said pooled. For teams it is the opposite: the
  mock is HONESTLY pooled, matching its own "Ranked across pooled leagues" intro line. 3 of its 4
  boards genuinely mix teams from one league (Real Madrid + Barcelona both La Liga on Goals/Shots
  on target/Passes; Arsenal + Manchester City both Premier League on Shots on target/Passes) and
  drop other pool leagues off the board entirely. Only Duels per match lands on 7 distinct leagues,
  by coincidence, not design. Reported to the CPO before asking, so the ruling below was made
  knowing the mock is not a free preview of it, unlike the players case.

  RULING 1 — ONE TEAM PER LEAGUE. CPO: "one team per league, same as players." Each pool league's
  rank-1 team on the metric, collected, then ordered by value — same mechanic as the players
  ruling, never a rank crossing `league_code`.

  WHAT THIS COSTS AND SAVES. Unlike players, this is NOT free: no team leaderboard mart exists yet
  (GAP-29, design approved 2026-08-08, still not started), and the CURRENT mock does not preview
  the ruled shape (see above) — it needs redoing before it previews the real design. What the
  ruling DOES settle: when GAP-29's mart is eventually built, partition the rank by (league_code,
  season_api_year, metric_key) — the way `mart_team_competition_benchmarks` already does — and the
  per-league rank is free, no pooled-across-leagues computation is ever needed, same mechanism as
  the players ruling. ⚠ CORRECTION while writing this up: GAP-29's row was first drafted saying "no
  gap was filed to withdraw here, since nobody had proposed a pooled rank for teams yet" — that is
  WRONG. GAP-31's own problem statement already said "**both** reduced blocks" rank across the pool
  (players AND teams) and its disposition explicitly named GAP-29 ("a separate pooled mart (which
  is also GAP-29's shape for teams, so the two should be settled together)"). GAP-31 was withdrawn
  for the PLAYERS half only when it was written (teams was still unruled then); today's ruling
  settles the teams half GAP-31 already raised. Caught by bi-analyst-reviewer in this same task's
