2026-08-25 feat/82-mr4b-generate-and-promote — #82 MR4b, the derived metric columns. FOUR ROUNDS,
SIX REAL DEFECTS, AND I FOUND ONE OF THEM. The one I found is the reason the MR exists.

  THE ONE I FOUND, by READING the output rather than checking it. Composing the first blocks
  showed `goals_against_sum_season` — a column that exists only on TEAM models — carrying the
  catalogue's `goals_against`, which the catalogue defines for a PLAYER: "goals conceded by the
  team while the player was on the pitch". 21 of 76 derived names had that shape. Nothing
  automatic would have caught it: the block resolves, the length is fine, the YAML parses, the
  hygiene gate sees a description where it wants one. It reaches the warehouse reading as
  authoritative.
  ⚠ THE OBVIOUS FIX WAS THE WRONG ONE. Teaching the generator which models are team-scoped is the
  classifier this repo has failed at three times. A fourth attempt, written only to measure the
  damage, left 2 of 12 models unresolved. So the generator was made UNABLE TO BE WRONG instead of
  cleverer: every derived block carries an entity suffix, whether or not its metric is split, and
  a metric the catalogue never defined for a column's entity has no block to point at. The column
  stays blank and VISIBLE rather than documented and wrong.
  ⭐ SEVEN METRICS ARE PLAYER-ONLY IN THE CATALOGUE WHILE TEAM MODELS USE THE SAME NAMES —
  `goals`, `goals_against`, `defensive_actions`, `shots_on_goal`, `shots_total`, `passes_accurate`,
  `passes_total`. 48 team columns therefore have no definition to point at and stay blank. THE
  CATALOGUE IS THE CPO'S; the question for him is whether these are team metrics the catalogue is
  missing, or team columns that are misnamed. football-analytics-expert-reviewer read it
  independently and judged them genuinely different quantities, not a misnaming. Filed as its own
  issue. Same family as #88, which he raised himself.

  ⛔⛔ THE PATTERN THAT MATTERS, AND IT IS ABOUT ME, NOT THE CODE. scope-auditor FAILED THREE ROUNDS
  RUNNING ON ONE DEFECT CLASS: each time it named a stale claim, each time I corrected exactly the
  sentence it named and nothing else, and each time the same claim was still sitting somewhere
  else in the same document. Round 1 the objective. Round 2 the blast_radius and two done_when
  bullets. Round 3 the blast_radius again, for a different reason. Its round-3 words are the whole
  lesson: "disclosed in an amendment is not the same as reflected in the impact map that a
  reviewer is told to trust for blast radius."
  ⚠ THE REPO ALREADY HAS THIS RULE — "corrections replace, never accumulate, and must replace
  EVERYWHERE" — and reading it did not stop me doing it three times in one MR. That is the fourth
  recorded failure of that prose rule. GitLab #71 is the mechanism it is waiting on.
  ⭐ WHAT ACTUALLY WORKED, on the fourth attempt: MEASURING instead of describing. Rendering both
  versions of the generated file and comparing them whitespace-normalised named the 3 affected
  blocks exactly and proved nothing else moved. It also corrected a number I had given two
  reviewers — I said "five pre-existing breaks" from counting LINES; three BLOCKS are affected.
  A described blast radius is a claim; a measured one is a fact.

  ⛔ MUTATION TESTING CHANGED THE DESIGN TWICE AND DELETED TWO GUARDS. Eleven mutations, eight
  killed, THREE SURVIVED, and every survivor was a real defect:
  · reversing the affix order changed nothing, because `_decompose` accepts only a stem that is a
    real metric — so the test could not fail, AND the rule was wrong. `goals_per_match_this_season`
    decomposes two ways, and longest-affix-first picks the LESS specific metric, silently dropping
    the null policy the catalogue wrote for that rate. Changed to LONGEST STEM WINS. ⭐ That fix
    closed 22 further columns, because they now resolve to a team-scoped rate instead of a
    player-only count.
  · deleting a dotted-name filter changed nothing: a dotted name cannot decompose at all, since the
    dot always lands in the stem and no metric id contains one. Dead guard, unfailable test.
    Replaced with a check on the EMITTED names, which is reachable and dies to its own mutation.
  · deleting a same-entity guard changed nothing: an earlier function already aborts on it. Both
    the guard and its test deleted rather than kept as decoration.
  ⚠ AND A TWELFTH MUTATION SURVIVED ITS AIMED TEST TOO. The hyphen test used a natural sentence
  containing "year-on-year", and at width 95 the wrapper never happened to choose that hyphen.
  Rewritten with a single token longer than the wrap width, which leaves it nowhere to hide.
  ⭐ FOUR OF MY OWN TESTS COULD NOT FAIL. Writing a test is not the same as having one.

  ⛔ A SHARED BLOCK IS ONLY AS TRUE AS ITS WIDEST CALL SITE, and I learned it twice in two rounds
  on the same sentence. Round 1: the year-on-year phrase claimed a NULL cause ("a gap in
  statistical coverage") that is real for a team and IMPOSSIBLE for a player, because a player's
  null per-match stat means ZERO. Round 2: my fix removed that false cause and took a TRUE one
  with it — the block is reused at `mart_player_profile`, which carries cup and tournament seasons
  where there is no year-on-year comparison at all. First a cause wrongly added, then a cause
  wrongly removed, both because I read ONE model and wrote a sentence attached to TWO.

  ⛔ `textwrap` HAD BEEN SPLITTING HYPHENATED WORDS, AND FIVE WERE ALREADY LIVE ON MAIN.
  `break_on_hyphens` defaults to True, so "year-on-year" became "year-on-" / "year". The file is
  not laid out for a reader of the file: `persist_docs` collapses the newline and the warehouse
  renders a space inside the term. `!98` shipped three such blocks. Found only because a test
  failed for the wrong reason.

  ⛔ A COMPOSED SENTENCE CAN CONTRADICT ITSELF AND PASS EVERYTHING. `clean_sheets` is a RATIO and
  its text carries that ratio's display convention ("e.g. 3/5"); `clean_sheets_sum_season` is the
  raw count. Composed, the block said the value was both a small fraction and a season total.
  Caught by analytics-engineer-reviewer READING it. Fixed as a class: a totalling affix is refused
  on any metric with a `denominator_expr`. ⚠ And the fix hid the column from the wiring tool, which
  reads "no candidate block" as "not a derived name" — so the generator now REPORTS every refusal.

  ⭐ ROUTING A REVIEWER BY JUDGEMENT RATHER THAN BY PATH CAUGHT TWO DEFECTS.
  `football-analytics-expert-reviewer`'s trigger is `metric_catalogue.csv`, which this MR never
  touches. It was invited because the MR composes prose making claims about how metrics are
  computed, and those claims reach 130 warehouse columns. It found the false player NULL cause and
  then the true one my fix dropped. THE TRIGGER NAMES A FILE; THE RISK IT EXISTS TO CATCH IS NOT
  ALWAYS IN THAT FILE.

  ⚠ platform-reviewer DISCLOSED, in three consecutive rounds, that it had no execution tools and
  had traced the code symbolically instead of running it. It re-derived every mutation claim by
  hand rather than believing the write-up, and in one round caught its own tool returning a stale
  read and re-checked three ways rather than reporting it. A reviewer stating the limits of its own
  method is worth more than one that does not.

  CPO DECISION, 2026-08-25: round 4 approved, over the cap of 3, asked as "a reviewer has caught
  the same mistake in my paperwork three times; I have fixed the third one and nobody has checked
  it — do I run one more check, or ship?"
  CPO ANSWER, verbatim: "run one more check".

  MEASURED: 130 columns closed, blank in-scope 635 to 508; 161 blocks (80 metric, 81 derived), all
  derived entity-suffixed; 48 columns reserved to the CPO and listed by the tool on every run;
