2026-08-27 #92 — THE MR DQ GATE WAS TESTING PRODUCTION, AND THE CPO APPROVED THE FIX.

  ⭐ HIS RULING, verbatim: **"do as recommended"**. What he was recommended, and what he was told it
  costs, both in the message he answered:
    · the fix — "one flag on one line tells that second run to read live data instead of what the
      branch just built. Remove it and it reads the branch"
    · why it is worth more than unblocking one MR — "all 30 of those checks currently test
      production, not your branch. They pass because production is fine, not because the change is"
    · the cost, stated before he answered — "those checks would then read from a workspace shared
      between branches, so they could pick up leftovers from another branch's run. Narrower hole
      than the one we have"
    · the alternative he did not take — renaming in two passes (add the new name beside the old,
      merge, let prod catch up, delete the old), which needs no CI change but doubles the batches
      and briefly puts two names on one number.
  He was also told it is a locked CI file needing his go and the two senior reviewers. This is the
  `protected_override` authority for `fix/92-singular-tests-read-the-branch`.

  ⛔ THE DEFECT, precisely, because "reads prod" is too loose to act on. `data:build:mr` has TWO dbt
  invocations. Both carried `--defer --favor-state`. `--favor-state` resolves every `ref()` to the
  DEFERRED (prod) relation EVEN WHEN THE CURRENT RUN JUST BUILT THAT MODEL.
    · on the `dbt build` line that is CORRECT and stays: a model being built must take its upstreams
      from prod, never from a stale `ci_` copy another MR left, and a node being built is not
      deferred anyway.
    · on the `dbt test` line nothing is being built, so the flag threw away the MR's own models and
      pointed all 30 singular tests at production.
  The two lines are now ASYMMETRIC ON PURPOSE, and the file says so at the point of difference.

  ⚠ THE FILE ASSERTED THE OPPOSITE, WHICH IS WHY THIS SURVIVED SO LONG. The comment above the test
  line read "validates this MR's rebuilt ci_* models layered over prod (via defer) — an ISOLATED
  view, not shared prod state". Exactly backwards for the rebuilt models, which is the half that
  matters. ⭐ THE RULE: when a guard misbehaves, suspect the comment that says it does not. A
  confident comment is why nobody re-derived the flag's semantics.

  ⭐ THE REPRODUCTION, one job, ninety seconds apart — pipeline 2796272877, job 16145201830, on !114:
    line 836  OK created sql table model ci_marts.mart_team_season_insights   (renamed column)
    line 854  PASS assert_mart_team_season_insights_metric_consistency        (inside the build)
    line 1021 Database Error ... Unrecognized name: points_capture_pct; Did you mean points_capture?
  Same assertion, same branch, same job. Only the database differed.

  ⛔ AND WHY IT COULD NOT BE WAITED OUT: prod gains the renamed column only after the rename merges,
  and the rename cannot merge while the gate is red. !114 could never go green on its own. Every
  column rename that a singular test names has this shape, and step 4 is 35 more renames.

  ⚠ WHAT #92 STILL HOLDS OPEN, so this entry is not read as closing it: `--defer` alone still
  prefers a `ci_` relation WHEN ONE EXISTS, so an UNMODIFIED upstream can be read from another MR's
  leftovers in the shared datasets. Narrower than aiming the whole suite at the wrong database, and
  it fails loudly rather than silently green. Closing it properly means per-MR ephemeral datasets —
  a RECURRING COST decision, and therefore the CPO's, not taken here.

  ⛔⛔ SAME DAY, SECOND HALF: THAT RESIDUAL FIRED ON THE FIRST PIPELINE, AND THE CPO RULED TWICE.
