  exists". Four humans found it by reading SQL. That is the second instance of the shape #96 already
  records — offline suite green, warehouse wrong — and the more serious one.

  ⛔⛔ **THE DEFECT, AND IT GOVERNS ALL SIX REMAINING BATCHES: A REFERENCE TO AN UPSTREAM COLUMN IS
  NOT THE METRIC'S OWN NAME.** These player metric names are also PROVIDER STAT WORDS — `shots_total`
  is the same identifier from `stg_apif__fixture_players.sql:51` through base, core and
  `int_legs__player_match`. So one word does two jobs, and a rename must move only one of them:
      sum(p.shots_total) as shots_total     -- READ of the leg column | WRITE of the metric
  The renamer moved both. `shots_on_goal` and `finishing_efficiency` escaped only because their
  upstream columns are `shots_on` and a formula — different tokens. **Measured: 20 of the 35 player
  metrics collide with an upstream column this way.**
  ⭐ **THE RULE THAT REPLACES IT — classify by SHAPE, not by file:**
    · **dotted** (`s.shots_total`) follows its SOURCE — it moves only if that relation renamed the
      column. All 14 in the player metric models were enumerated: 12 read a metric relation and
      move, 2 read a provider relation and do not.
    · **alias** (`... as X`) is the metric being written. Always moves. 13 sites.
    · **bare** is an upstream read when it sits inside the aggregate that aliases the SAME name
      (`sum(X) [over w] as X`), otherwise this model's own column. 4 protected, 30 moved.
  ⚠ MY FIRST VERSION OF THAT RULE PROTECTED **ALL** DOTTED READS, which would have left
  `mart_player_profile` and `int_player_profile__yoy` reading columns their own upstream had just
  renamed. Caught by the yml-column guard — which had itself to be fixed first, because it compared
  the new yml name against the PRE-rename SQL on disk instead of the post-transform state.

