2026-09-01 ROUND 1 FAILED TWICE, INDEPENDENTLY, AND THE UNIT OF THE RULE WAS WRONG.

  ⛔ `scope-auditor` and `analytics-engineer` both FAILed the seed-prose sweep, on the same defect,
  reached separately. The rule was per-CELL: convert a cell when every occurrence inside it is
  movable. That moves whichever COLUMN happens to hold a convertible phrase and freezes its sibling
  — so a row ends up reading **"on goal" in its label, "on goal" in its description and "on target"
  in its interpretation**.

  ⭐⭐ THAT IS NOT AN APPROXIMATION OF THE CPO's RULING. IT IS THE LITERAL STATE HE NAMED. His words,
  logged in the entry above as this MR's authority: *"Otherwise one metric ends up saying 'on goal'
  in its label, 'on goal' in its description, and 'on target' in its interpretation."* I quoted that
  sentence in `contract.md` as the reason for the two-column scope, and then built a rule that
  produces it. **Quoting a ruling is not the same as implementing it, and nothing in my process
  checks the difference.**

  ⚠ SCALE, and it is why this was a FAIL and not a nit: `analytics-engineer` measured **7 rows**
  where two previously-AGREEING columns were made to disagree, and traced it to a live artifact —
  `fetch_glossary()` (`scripts/export_site_data.py:1296`) serialises `description` and
  `interpretation` side by side into `metrics.json`, so the disagreement ships verbatim.

  ⭐ THE FIX WAS TO CHANGE THE UNIT, NOT TO ADD AN EXCEPTION. A row's cells are written only if the
  ROW ends fully consistent; otherwise nothing on that row is touched. **A partly-converted ROW is
  worse than an unconverted one, for exactly the reason a partly-converted CELL is** — the rule was
  already in the contract, one level down, and simply never lifted.

  ⚠ AND AN INTERMEDIATE FIX WAS ALSO WRONG, which is worth recording because it looked right. My
  first correction guarded "do not NEWLY split a row" — and it passed those 7 rows, because their
  LABEL already split them on main. A guard phrased against "getting worse" is satisfied by
  something already bad. The condition has to be **the end state is right**, not **the delta is
  not negative**.

  ⛔ THE COST IS REAL AND IS NOT BEING HIDDEN: the sweep shrank from **12 substitutions to 5**, and
  the MR no longer fixes its own headline example — `shots_on_goal_player` still reads label
  "Shots on goal" / description "Shots on target.", because its interpretation says "On-target
  threat". **Seven rows are now blocked on one COPY decision**, which is the CPO's under the copy
  gate: what replaces **"On-target threat"**, **"On-target dominance"**, **"On-target shots faced"**,
  **"On-target threat conceded"** and "the on-target process". "On-goal threat" is not English.

  ⭐ FOURTH REGEX LESSON ON ONE FILE, and this one is new in kind. The three before were about
  detecting the OLD word. This one is about the NEW one: the row-consistency check first asked
  `"on goal" in cell` — a literal SPACE — so a cell converted to *"shots-on-goal data"* read as
  still-unconverted, and `deserved_points` scored as a clean move while its row actually ended
  holding both spellings. **The detector for what you are WRITING needs exactly the permissiveness
  of the detector for what you are REPLACING.** I had already written the permissive-counting rule
  into this contract for `on target` and did not apply it to `on goal` in the very next function.


