2026-09-06 — fix/deserved-points-clamped-to-legal-range — DESERVED POINTS, CAP NOT WITHHOLD
================================================================================

CONTEXT. `int_team_season_deserved_points_in_legal_range` failed the prod build on the !153 merge
pipeline (job 16331792666). One row: LP 2026, four games played, a fitted 12.0259 points against a
ceiling of 3 x 4 = 12. It is severity=error inside `dbt build`, so ~620 downstream nodes were marked
skipped and prod stopped refreshing below that model's subtree.

MY RECOMMENDATION, WHICH HE REJECTED. I put three options to him and recommended the second:
  1. Raise the fittability gate above 3 finished games.
  2. Withhold the whole league-season's fit whenever any team leaves [0, 3 * games].
  3. Clamp the value into range.
My argument for 2 was that an out-of-range value is evidence the least-squares LINE is wrong rather
than that one team's number needs trimming, so clamping would hide a degenerate fit behind a
legal-looking number and leave 17 quietly-wrong teammates on the same bad slope.

HIS RULING, verbatim:
  "Definitely no! We need to find a smarter way. In the example the deserved points would be 12 and
   12.0259. The latter is the result of the estimation. We could use methods that count for
   censoring of the dependent variable but to be pragmatic, we use OLS and cap mechanically at the
   possible max or mins. Makes sense? Or not?"

and, on the outcome's domain:
  "Deserved points is allways a positive integer incl zero"

WHY HE WAS RIGHT, measured rather than conceded. Over the 1,125 fitted rows in prod: exactly ONE
crosses a bound, by 0.0065 points per match (3.0065 against a ceiling of 3.0); none crosses the
floor; the minimum fitted rate is 0.1944; and NO other row is within 0.1 of either bound. The
degenerate-fit population my objection assumed does not exist in the data. A linear predictor on a
bounded outcome leaving its support by a rounding-scale amount is an expected property, not a
symptom — which is the framing his "censoring of the dependent variable" names exactly.

WHAT THE INTEGER RULING SETTLES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It settles the DISPLAY, and the display
already honoured it before this MR: `DeservedHero.astro:79` renders `integer(s.deserved_points)`, so
no reader has ever seen 12.0259. It does NOT move rounding into the warehouse, and that was put to
him with the measurement: rounding the stored column takes rows tied on `deserved_points` from 75 of
1,125 to 252 of 1,125, so more than a fifth of teams would share a `deserved_rank` as an artifact of
rounding rather than of the fit, and it scatters the hero's dots off the trend line they are
colinear with. He said "go ahead" on the split as stated — warehouse continuous and bounded, page
integer.

⚠ WHAT HE DID NOT RULE. Nothing about WHEN this read should be shown. The two mid-season questions
recorded in memory — how to render the fitted line mid-season, and from which matchday to show it at
all — are untouched and remain open. Raising a matchday threshold later is still fully available;
this MR deliberately does not spend it.

